# Phase 3 (lex ';' and '..<'): '..<' is now a single three-character token
# (TK_DOTLT, lexed at src/tychoc.c:482, maximal-munch alongside '...') but has
# no grammar yet. It is destined for `parallel for i in 0..<N:` only -- a
# SEQUENTIAL `for i in 0..<N:` is refused deliberately and stays refused after
# phase 5 (see plan.md's Pre-flight: allowing it would be `range()` under a new
# name). Today the refusal comes from the foreach branch of the `for` parser,
# which parses `0` as the collection expression and then demands the block
# colon at src/tychoc.c:3359:
#
#     error: expected ':' before the block
#
# with the caret on the '..<'. Phase 5 replaces this with a diagnostic that
# names the three-clause form as the sequential replacement.
#
# NOTE this one did NOT die in the lexer before phase 3: `0..<3` lexed as
# INT '.' '.' '<' INT, and the parser refused it at the postfix-field site with
# "expected a field name or tuple index after '.'". What phase 3 changes is
# WHICH token the parser sees, not that it is the parser doing the refusing.
fn main():
    for i in 0..<3:
        print(str(i))
